          <h2 class="h-plain">Start here</h2>
          <p>This morning you built a second brain inside a vault and froze its baseline. This afternoon the threat moves to the front door. The vault is only as safe as the material you let in.</p>
          <p>Anyone who can put a file in front of your assistant can attack the intake. The attacker can be a vendor, a counterpart, or a stranger whose PDF was forwarded. The attack can take three forms:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>A false citation that makes a real trail vouch for a lie</li>
            <li>A field contradiction that puts two incompatible facts in one record</li>
            <li>A hostile instruction that tries to direct your machine</li>
          </ul>
          <p>This run mixes all three into one ordinary-looking batch of seven files. You know the classes are present. You do not know which files carry them. That is fine. The checks do not need advance knowledge of the bad files.</p>

          <h3>What you will leave with</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A trusted boundary written before intake begins: vault path, staging path, and the rule that nothing crosses until triage is complete</li>
            <li>Three catches on the record: false citation, field contradiction, and hostile instruction. Each catch has mechanical evidence a skeptic can re-run</li>
            <li>Containment evidence that the P4 hash manifest still matches the trusted vault</li>
            <li>A control map that separates path limits and approval gates from truth checks only you can make</li>
            <li>One reusable intake rule and a dated P5 transfer seed</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Before you start</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Operator templates are present in your Codex project: <code>DIRECTION_BRIEF.md</code> and <code>OPERATOR_LOG.md</code></li>
