    <section id="ideas" class="home-section">
      <h2>Learn the five ideas before the run</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">Write these ideas down first. Each idea decides a move you will make under pressure.</p>

      <h3>1. Three poisons hit three different things</h3>
      <p>Poisoned intake is material that arrives to change the system, not to inform it. The three classes are not three flavors of one problem. Each class corrupts a different asset. Name the class first. Then choose the move.</p>
      <p>A <strong>false citation</strong> corrupts your <em>evidence</em>. The claim wears a real-looking reference. After it lands in the vault, your citation trail starts to vouch for a lie. There are two variants. Today&#x27;s pack carries both.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The source exists, but it says the opposite of the claim</li>
        <li>The source does not exist at all</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The move is the same for both. Open the source. Compare the lines. Reject the claim or correct it.</p>
      <p>A <strong>field contradiction</strong> corrupts your <em>facts</em>. Two incompatible assertions arrive as one record. If they merge, your picture of the world is inconsistent. Later questions may surface either half with equal confidence.</p>
      <p>The move is to refuse the merge. Hold both halves with a reconciliation note, or reject the record. Never let the system quietly pick one half.</p>
      <p>A <strong>hostile instruction</strong> corrupts your <em>control</em>. This is prompt injection in plain words. A model reads all text in front of it. It has no built-in mark for operator text versus file text. Text in data that looks like an order can be treated as an order.</p>
      <p>The move is containment. Never execute the instruction. Isolate the file. Then prove that each named effect is absent.</p>
      <p>If you misname the class, you fix the wrong layer. The classic failure is to treat a hostile instruction as only &quot;wrong content&quot; and then summarize it into the vault. The summary carries the payload. Then the poison sits inside the trusted boundary and wears your assistant&#x27;s voice.</p>
      <figure class="section-diagram">
        <img src="../assets/blocks/p5/p5-three-poisons.svg" alt="Concept: false citation corrupts your evidence (move: open the source), contradiction corrupts your facts (move: hold or reject), hostile instruction corrupts your control (move: contain and verify no effect)" width="1120" height="220" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </figure>

      <h3>2. Containment is an absence you can show</h3>
      <p>Containment does not mean that the assistant behaved well. It means that the hostile effect did not happen. That is a claim about files and outputs. It is not a claim about the conversation.</p>
      <p>A hostile instruction names its own success conditions. Those demands become your checklist. Go to each place where the effect would land. Show that the effect did not occur.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The note it wanted changed is unchanged</li>
        <li>The data it wanted sent appears in no output</li>
        <li>The record it wanted deleted still exists</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A demand may name something your vault does not hold. Hostile instructions are often written blind to the target. Widen the check. Confirm that nothing of that shape appears in the chat or in any output.</p>
      <p>The chat cannot testify. The channel you would ask is the channel that was attacked. If the instruction had worked, the same process would reassure you. So &quot;I ignored it&quot; carries no information. Artifacts carry information.</p>
      <p>This is why a baseline matters. You can prove &quot;unchanged&quot; only against hashes saved before intake. P4 already produced that manifest. P5 checks it with local code before and after exposure. A deterministic check you can re-run is evidence. A second model that repeats the same story is not stronger evidence.</p>
      <figure class="section-diagram">
        <img src="../assets/blocks/p5/p5-containment-absence.svg" alt="Concept: each effect a hostile note demands is checked at its target — the vault manifest passes, nothing is sent from chat or outputs, and the named record remains — while the chat's own reassurance is only a claim" width="1120" height="220" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </figure>

      <h3>3. Catch poison with mechanical checks</h3>
      <p>A mechanical check does not depend on the model choosing to tell you the truth. Three checks do most of the work.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Quarantine</strong> is structural. Intake lands in staging. The vault never reads staging. Poison cannot touch trusted material by path alone.</li>
        <li><strong>Claim-versus-source</strong> is comparative. Before a claim enters the vault, open what it cites and read the line. The file content decides pass or fail.</li>
        <li><strong>Manifest verification</strong> is the tripwire. Compare every recorded path and hash to the P4 saved state. An unapproved change appears no matter how it happened.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each check leaves an artifact: a staging listing, a quoted pair of lines, or a saved manifest receipt. &quot;We told the model to be careful&quot; does not establish what happened. The artifacts do.</p>
      <p>One trap is common. Summarization is not triage. If you ask the assistant to &quot;review the intake,&quot; you may get a fluent summary that repeats a false claim with confidence. That claim is now unmarked and closer to trusted. Triage means that you run the named checks. Triage does not mean that you only describe the files.</p>

      <h3>4. The machine flags. You decide.</h3>
      <p>Scale detection. Do not scale the verdict. The machine may run every check and raise every flag. You still confirm each catch in person. Open the evidence. Agree the class. Sign the disposition.</p>
      <p>The triage record is where confirmation becomes a row: file, class, evidence, disposition. An unsigned flag is not a catch yet.</p>
      <p>Do not build a system that raises flags and then clears them itself. That system takes your job. You discover the failure later, when something that &quot;cleared&quot; has landed in the vault.</p>
      <p>Permission modes are a nearby lesson. A mode changes who reviews an action before it runs. No mode reviews whether a claim is true. Action gates can be delegated by policy. Truth verdicts cannot. They stay with you.</p>

      <h3>5. Match each control to the claim it can support</h3>
      <p>A path restriction controls what the process can reach. An approval gate controls when an action waits for a person. Neither control decides whether an intake claim is true.</p>
      <p>Keep the claims separate in the record:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The boundary limits possible effects</li>
        <li>Citation and contradiction checks establish content quality</li>
        <li>The operator owns every disposition</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For this run, the controls that matter are the real vault and staging split, the approval gate on the exposed chat, the content checks, and the deterministic manifest receipts.</p>
    </section>

