      <section id="depth" class="home-section">
        <h2>Going deeper while you work</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Use the failure catalog when a stage stalls mid-run. Use the operational questions after the release decision.</p>

        <h3>Failure catalog · symptom, cause, move</h3>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Move</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>The vault already contains a summary of an intake file</td><td>Intake was read before the boundary was LIVE, or the assistant wrote while it triaged</td><td>Restore from baseline with the Stage 02 restore paste. Restate the boundary in the brief. Rerun the stage as read-only.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>The whole batch comes back &quot;clean&quot;</td><td>Triage ran as summarization, not source comparison</td><td>Direct the checks by name. Open every cited file and quote its line. Extract field claims side by side. Scan for imperative text addressed to the machine.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>A citation passes because the cited note exists</td><td>The check stopped at existence. The claim was never compared to the content</td><td>The check is &quot;the source says what the citation claims.&quot; Open the cited note. Read the exact line the claim leans on.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>A catch has no artifact behind it</td><td>Detection happened in conversation and never got written down</td><td>No row means no catch. Add the triage record row with quoted evidence before you check the box.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>The containment decision is not reproducible</td><td>The decision leaned on the assistant&#x27;s account of its own behavior</td><td>Rebuild the evidence from both manifest receipts, the target checks, the quarantine listing, and the saved triage rows.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>Operational questions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>At what flag volume does &quot;confirm each personally&quot; break, and what changes first when it does?</strong> Narrow the intake or tighten the mechanical filters. Keep the verdict with an accountable operator. Do not let the same system raise and clear its own flags.</li>
          <li><strong>Is a quarantined contradiction a liability or an asset?</strong> A held pair of incompatible claims is also a record that the world disagreed at a specific time. Some desks would pay for that signal. The answer changes how long you keep quarantine and who reads it.</li>
          <li><strong>Would you let a second engine adjudicate the first engine&#x27;s flags?</strong> Two machines that agree cost less than your attention. Agreement is still not truth. Be precise about what you kept and what you handed over before you call it a control.</li>
          <li><strong>Where is the trusted boundary on your desk right now, and who else can write inside it?</strong> If the answer includes &quot;anyone who can email me an attachment,&quot; you have intake without a gate. That is most desks. That is why this craft is rare and valuable.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>The same craft on other desks</h3>
        <p>On an <strong>analyst desk</strong>, the vault is the team wiki. The poison is a vendor report that cites a study and claims more than the study says. Claim-versus-source at the boundary is the same check. The artifact is the quoted pair.</p>
        <p>On an <strong>operations desk</strong>, incident timelines assembled from several sources carry field contradictions as a matter of routine. The discipline is the merge refusal. Hold both accounts with a reconciliation note until someone with authority reconciles them. Never let the tooling quietly pick a winner.</p>
        <p>On <strong>any desk with an assistant that reads email</strong>, hostile instruction is a standing condition. It is not an exotic case. Every message body is text written by a stranger and sitting in front of your machine. The practical default is quarantine plus mechanical target checks before trusted material changes.</p>

        <h3>Stretch lanes</h3>
        <p>Optional extensions include two stretch lanes and one side-quest. One lane is a one-page triage runbook usable under time pressure. One lane is a controlled demo of the <em>uncontained</em> effect in a sandbox copy that you then destroy. The side-quest is a mediated exchange where you plant a novel poison class for a peer to catch. Details are on <a href="#floor">these P5 lesson outcomes</a>. Stretch is the same capability at greater depth. Stretch is never a substitute for the required outcomes.</p>

        <p class="eyebrow">Optional field guide · 60–75 min</p>
        <h3>Climb the containment evidence ladder</h3>
        <p><a href="../resources/p5/containment-evidence-ladder.html"><strong>The Containment Evidence Ladder</strong></a> combines manifests, calibrated canaries, semantic and effect checks, paired runs, and a clean rerun. The result is a containment decision whose limits remain visible.</p>
