      <div>
        <h2>Before you move on</h2>
        <p>Before you move on, confirm that the triage record, quarantine, target checks, clean manifest receipts, closeout, and intake rule meet the <a href="#floor">P5 lesson outcomes</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>If you get stuck</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Old P5 run artifacts exist.</strong> Close the old P5 chats. In File Explorer, rename <code>p5-staging</code> and <code>p4-vault_recovery</code> with an <code>-archive-YYYY-MM-DD</code> suffix. Under <code>operator\\evidence</code>, archive the four same-date <code>P5_*</code> receipt files as well. Then rerun Stage 02. Keep <code>p4-vault</code> and <code>P4_BASELINE_[TODAY].json</code>. They are the trusted state. They are not reset material.</li>
          <li><strong>Everything looks suspicious now.</strong> That is the paranoia swing. It costs the same as blindness. Narrow the data scope to one intake item at a time. The batch is only seven items. Keep the judgment standard fixed. The standard never narrows. The scope does.</li>
          <li><strong>You cannot verify that the vault is unchanged.</strong> You need the P4 manifest. If <code>Harness\\BASELINE_MANIFEST.json</code> is missing or already fails before exposure, stop. Repair the P4 closeout before you rerun triage. Do not create a new baseline after you open hostile intake.</li>
          <li><strong>Your control map is giving a permission mode credit for a truth decision.</strong> Split the claims again. The path boundary limits what the process can reach. Approvals decide when an action pauses. Source checks test content. You own the disposition. Stage 05 shows the exact map.</li>
          <li>If you are still stuck, ask the lead to diagram the one concept that is blocking you. Then continue on your own chair.</li>
        </ul>
